#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064002 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064247 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f6a7917-f877-5263-abea-a78c0707d9fc" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Trinomial With Positive Coefficients"}] :question/id #uuid "44cf8c85-f228-5eaa-922d-613355cb7d5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-15070" :question/problem "Factor the quadratic expression $10a^2 + 11a + 3.$" :question/worked-solution "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n10a^2 + 11a + 3.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($10 \\times 3 = 30$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($11$).\n\nSince all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the positive factor pairs of $30.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $30$ are\n\n$$\n(1,30), \\quad (2,15), \\quad (3,10), \\quad (5,6).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(5,6)$ add to $11{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5+6 & =11\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $5a + 6a,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10{a}^{2}+11a+3 & = \\\\ 10{a}^{2}+5a+6a+3 & = \\\\ (10{a}^{2}+5a)+(6a+3) & = \\\\ 5a(2a+1)+3(2a+1) & = \\\\ (5a+3)(2a+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064003 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064249 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d5115f8-05b7-5df5-92b1-b4faa95f8449" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Trinomials With Negative Coefficients and Positive Constants"}] :question/id #uuid "ad032833-ecf8-5295-9a9f-f69f759c19b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-15072" :question/problem "Factor the expression $3x^2 - 16x + 16.$" :question/worked-solution "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n3x^2 - 16x + 16.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($3 \\times 16 = 48$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($-16$).\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the product of the quadratic coefficient and constant is positive, we only need to consider the negative factor pairs of $48.$\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $48$ are\n\n$$\n(-1,-48), \\quad (-2,-24), \\quad (-3,-16), \\quad (-4,-12), \\quad (-6,-8).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-4,-12)$ add to $-16{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)+(-12) & =-16\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-4x - 12x,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-16x+16 & = \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-4x-12x+16 & = \\\\ (3{x}^{2}-4x)-(12x-16) & = \\\\ x(3x-4)-4(3x-4) & = \\\\ (x-4)(3x-4) & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064004 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064248 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8cfe2121-66f0-53fa-b744-041fe830bf13" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Trinomials With Negative Constants"}] :question/id #uuid "35bedd9f-bcb7-5024-bfe7-5ef6d34518bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-15073" :question/problem "Factor the quadratic expression $2c^2 + 15c - 8.$" :question/worked-solution "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n2c^2 + 15c - 8.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2 \\times -8 = -16$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($15$).\n\nFirst, we find the factor pairs of $-16{:}$\n\n$$\n(1,-16),\\quad (-1,16), \\quad (2,-8), \\quad (-2,8), \\quad (4,-4)\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-1,16)$ add to $15{:}$ \n\n$$\n(-1) + 16 = 15 \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, rewrite the linear term as $- c+16c,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{c}^{2}+15c-8 & = \\\\ 2{c}^{2}-c+16c-8 & = \\\\ (2{c}^{2}-c)+(16c-8) & = \\\\ c(2c-1)+8(2c-1) & = \\\\ (2c-1)(c+8) & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064247 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f6a7917-f877-5263-abea-a78c0707d9fc" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Trinomial With Positive Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f956e5b-1886-5c73-b8bc-cf07f5514c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6w+1)(w+10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482836} {:answer/id #uuid "139e2599-d442-5bcf-9fed-9beb770bdac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3w+5)(2w+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482846} {:answer/id #uuid "47b0fd5d-4c37-52f2-9295-22a8f59254c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(3w+1)(w+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482940} {:answer/id #uuid "a8505418-0dab-57f8-904d-143d5e551a21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3w+10)(2w+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483097} {:answer/id #uuid "dfbd19af-9737-539e-b11a-593a8a2fa762" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3w+2)(2w+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfbd19af-9737-539e-b11a-593a8a2fa762" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3w+2)(2w+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483189} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5de56ee-fdfa-5ee6-87f8-0226508bf6e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "acf95ff0-c23c-5538-9064-3e039f763a54" :question/math-academy-id "q-111835" :question/problem "$6{w}^{2}+19w+10=$" :question/worked-solution "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n6{w}^{2}+19w+10.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($6\\times 10=60$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($19$).\n\nSince all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the positive factor pairs of $60.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $60$ are\n\n$$\n(1,60),\\,(2,30),\\,(3,10),\\,(4,15),\\,(5,12),\\,(6,10).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(4,15)$ add to $19:$ \n\n$$\n4+15=19\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $4w+15w,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{w}^{2}+19w+10 & = \\\\ 6{w}^{2}+4w+15w+10 & = \\\\ (6{w}^{2}+4w)+(15w+10) & = \\\\ 2w(3w+2)+5(3w+2) & = \\\\ (3w+2)(2w+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483430 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064247 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f6a7917-f877-5263-abea-a78c0707d9fc" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Trinomial With Positive Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0617ff8b-9a53-5a85-9776-ca14a6b5a1e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5m+1)(m+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482821} {:answer/id #uuid "3f3f1441-07e1-5f0d-b9d6-5cbf147a0251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5m+4)(2m+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482919} {:answer/id #uuid "a9dba60b-09d4-5767-814a-20971c8a355a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(5m+2)(m+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483099} {:answer/id #uuid "c51f24dc-3a4d-52e8-b926-ba0e14c2c0e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(10m+1)(m+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483140} {:answer/id #uuid "d5ca640d-8f6e-53a9-b58a-e4f953665ac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(5m+1)(m+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f3f1441-07e1-5f0d-b9d6-5cbf147a0251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5m+4)(2m+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482919} :answer-field/id #uuid "baaf1380-7960-5d0a-861f-dfdf49029a8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8a011fe-bff7-586f-86e9-fabb797df0e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-111838" :question/problem "$10{m}^{2}+13m+4=$" :question/worked-solution "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n10{m}^{2}+13m+4.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($10\\times 4=40$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($13$).\n\nSince all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the positive factor pairs of $40.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $40$ are\n\n$$\n(1,40),\\,(2,20),\\,(4,10),\\,(5,8).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(5,8)$ add to $13:$ \n\n$$\n5+8=13\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $5m+8m,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10{m}^{2}+13m+4 & = \\\\ 10{m}^{2}+5m+8m+4 & = \\\\ (10{m}^{2}+5m)+(8m+4) & = \\\\ 5m(2m+1)+4(2m+1) & = \\\\ (5m+4)(2m+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483361 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064249 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d5115f8-05b7-5df5-92b1-b4faa95f8449" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Trinomials With Negative Coefficients and Positive Constants"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fb21ada-6744-5f70-b1dc-ecaec4ba734e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fb21ada-6744-5f70-b1dc-ecaec4ba734e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482872} :answer-field/id #uuid "36a2e263-cd65-5517-af18-d6768ff0e51b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483257} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5050617d-d60e-5d12-93de-50f83c35fc74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5050617d-d60e-5d12-93de-50f83c35fc74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482955} :answer-field/id #uuid "4894e318-1ff2-5f50-b281-d62b33594a63" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483262} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0647f1f-8929-5a4c-a972-69473a57b03a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0647f1f-8929-5a4c-a972-69473a57b03a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483111} :answer-field/id #uuid "d93f8853-6923-594d-a9b4-a58c020bf26c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b50cd1f-80a5-51f1-b3eb-2663a6e28812" :question/math-academy-id "q-224897" :question/problem "Factor the following quadratic expression.\n\n$20{b}^{2}-12b+1=(2b\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)($\n{{field-2}}$b\\,-$\n{{field-3}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n20{b}^{2}-12b+1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($20\\times 1=20$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($-12$).\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the product of the quadratic coefficient and constant is positive, we only need to consider the negative factor pairs of $20.$\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $20$ are\n\n$$\n(-1,-20),\\,(-2,-10),\\,(-4,-5).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-2,-10)$ add to $-12:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2)+(-10) & =-12\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-2b-10b,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{b}^{2}-12b+1 & = \\\\ 20{b}^{2}-2b-10b+1 & = \\\\ (20{b}^{2}-2b)-(10b-1) & = \\\\ 2b(10b-1)-(10b-1) & = \\\\ (2b-1)(10b-1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064249 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d5115f8-05b7-5df5-92b1-b4faa95f8449" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Trinomials With Negative Coefficients and Positive Constants"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60d12287-da2e-5723-9891-def9ab5606f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60d12287-da2e-5723-9891-def9ab5606f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482982} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2c88bae-0e5d-514c-bf54-54c3a911e7e4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061768} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dd80e3a-05e9-5e40-a54c-e5cfea2ecc75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7dd80e3a-05e9-5e40-a54c-e5cfea2ecc75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483034} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f8e7b90-bd74-5dd6-b27c-58a4dbac0a72" :answe